680.43(B)(1) has three tiers for luminaires, lighting outlets and paddle fans over the spa or within 5 ft of it:
- at least 12 ft above maximum water level with no GFCI;
- at least 7 ft 6 in with GFCI (any type);
- below 7 ft 6 in only if GFCI-protected AND either recessed with a glass or plastic lens and nonmetallic or isolated trim, or surface-mounted with a glass or plastic globe and nonmetallic or isolated body, suitable for damp locations.
The report merged the second and third tiers. The receptacle at 6-10 ft, GFCI within 10 ft, switches at least 5 ft, and bonding of metal within 5 ft are as stated. The perimeter-bonding exception applies only to a listed self-contained spa above a finished floor. Confirm against the 2025 CEC.

The primary-heater rule, cover rule and pilot ban are confirmed in the CEC FAQ. 'Only with on-site renewables' overstates it: the same FAQ allows 'A heat pump pool heater ... with a supplementary heater, if needed, of any energy source.' The renewables wording attaches to resistance or gas used outside that heat-pump path. Exceptions include:
- portable electric spas that comply with Title 20;
- permanent-spa heating in existing buildings where gas is available;
- permanent spas in buildings with too little Solar Access Roof Area (SARA);
- alterations with existing heaters.
Confirm the supplementary-heater path with the plan checker.
